WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:05.220
To get an idea of how hard it is to land Starship,
it’s difficult enough landing SpaceX’s

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:07.660
much smaller rocket, the Falcon 9.

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:12.070
As one of SpaceX’s commentators put it,
landing the Falcon 9’s booster is similar

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:16.200
to launching a pencil over the Empire State
Building, putting it in reverse, and having

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.720
it come back down and land successfully.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:22.610
Or put another way, preparing the rocket
for re-entry to land vertically is like trying

00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:26.820
to balance a broomstick on your hand in the
middle of a windstorm

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:29.400
which is clearly very hard to do.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:34.280
Now take the difficulties of the Falcon 9
but multiply them and you get Starship.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:36.160
The largest rocket ever.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:40.430
As Starship prepares to send people to Mars
and back, SpaceX has to figure out how to

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:42.180
land this beast safely.

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:45.510
Starship enters Earth’s atmosphere in a
rather odd way.

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:49.010
It falls from the sky belly first after shutting
off the engines.

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:51.860
The belly flop helps decrease the velocity
of Starship.

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:55.630
The larger surface area increases air resistance
to slow its fall.

00:00:55.630 --> 00:01:00.120
Kind of like how a skydiver maintains a horizontal
body position with their arms and legs spread

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:01.720
to control their freefall.

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:04.850
The arms and legs of the Starship are its
four flaps.

00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:09.350
It then does a short flip to revert back to
a vertical position and reignites its Raptor

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:11.610
engines to get ready for landing.

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:16.140
In the case of prototype number 8, the vehicle
came in way too fast and blew apart when it

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:17.549
hit the ground.

00:01:17.549 --> 00:01:19.490
SpaceX had more luck with later versions.

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:24.340
So, why doesn’t this thing just land vertically
like the booster of the Falcon 9?

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:27.880
Landing the Falcon 9 booster is easier because
it doesn’t go into orbit.

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:32.159
It lifts the upper stage into orbit, before
returning to Earth within minutes from a relatively

00:01:32.159 --> 00:01:33.600
lower altitude.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.920
So it never achieves super-high speeds.

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:40.780
Whereas Starship goes into orbit and travels
much faster than the Falcon 9’s booster.

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:45.720
SpaceX’s biggest hopes and dreams rest on
this spacecraft sending 100 people or 100

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:47.280
tonnes to Mars.

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:49.060
This is how the trip would be made:

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:53.460
Starship is lifted into orbit by the Super
Heavy booster, which then comes back to Earth.

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:57.659
It meets up with another Starship configured
as a tanker in order to refuel in Earth’s

00:01:57.659 --> 00:02:00.660
orbit before making the long journey to the
red planet.

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:03.100
It could also fly to the moon in a similar
manner.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:07.009
The booster isn’t required for the return
since there’s no deep gravity well to escape

00:02:07.009 --> 00:02:08.160
from on Mars.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:11.529
Starship flies solo, fueled by locally produced
methane.

00:02:11.529 --> 00:02:13.590
Every step is a feat in itself.

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:18.080
But one of the most difficult is landing heavy
payloads on the surface of Mars.

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:21.459
It’s really, really, really hard to do.

00:02:21.459 --> 00:02:25.760
Only about half of all missions to Mars have
worked out. The ones that failed either missed

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:29.209
the planet altogether, crash-landed, or their
signal was cut.

00:02:29.209 --> 00:02:33.340
It’s much harder to land on Mars than on
Earth where our atmosphere allows the spacecraft

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:35.420
to glide gently down to the surface.

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:40.569
There won’t be anything like that on the
red planet - the atmosphere is too thin.

00:02:40.569 --> 00:02:42.040
100 times thinner than Earth’s.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:45.059
So it won’t be able to slow the vehicle
down.

00:02:45.059 --> 00:02:50.359
Starship will plummet toward the surface of
Mars at 7.5 km per second or 4 and a half

00:02:50.359 --> 00:02:51.830
miles a second.

00:02:51.830 --> 00:02:54.909
The heat shield will protect Starship from
melting during atmospheric entry.

00:02:54.909 --> 00:03:00.989
It’s made up of impressive hexagonal tiles
and is designed for multiple entries.

00:03:00.989 --> 00:03:06.510
Tests have shown these hex tiles can withstand
temperatures of 2500 degrees Fahrenheit or

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:09.059
about 1400 degrees Celsius.

00:03:09.059 --> 00:03:12.969
Because Starship is coming in so hot there
will be wear and tear just like the brake

00:03:12.969 --> 00:03:14.129
pads of a car.

00:03:14.129 --> 00:03:18.209
And, by the way, while this is happening,
there’s a communication blackout.

00:03:18.209 --> 00:03:21.819
Those on Earth won’t be able to talk to
Starship because of the long time delay due

00:03:21.819 --> 00:03:27.249
to distance - anywhere from 5 to 20 minutes
- so scientists can’t send commands in real-time.

00:03:27.249 --> 00:03:30.989
Instead, Starship will be pre-programmed with
a set of actions.

00:03:30.989 --> 00:03:34.859
It’ll be a flying robot that must rely on
its computers to land.

00:03:34.859 --> 00:03:39.949
Here at Newsthink, we’ve imagined what Starship’s
landing legs might look like as SpaceX engineers

00:03:39.949 --> 00:03:42.109
continue to refine their design.

00:03:42.109 --> 00:03:46.099
We know they will be retractable - tucked
into the rocket to minimize air resistance

00:03:46.099 --> 00:03:49.430
- and then spread out to maximize stability
for landing.

00:03:49.430 --> 00:03:53.790
Musk has said the legs will be automatically
leveled to handle the uneven, rocky terrain.

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:58.159
They will also have to be tough enough to
withstand pummelling by massive dust storms

00:03:58.159 --> 00:03:59.159
and radiation.

00:03:59.159 --> 00:04:03.040
We’ve removed the dust from this particular
shot so you can get a better look at the legs.

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:06.319
They may not be needed to land back on Earth,
however.

00:04:06.319 --> 00:04:10.620
SpaceX has the idea of catching Starship using
the launch tower with a grabbing arm called

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:15.120
Mechazilla - likely in reference to the villain
Mechagodzilla that confronts Godzilla in the

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:16.660
film franchise.

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:20.630
Dutch animator Tijn imagined what catching
Starship might look like, before stacking

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:24.680
it together with the Super Heavy booster to
get it ready for another flight.

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:29.509
This still untested strategy would significantly
reduce the turnaround time for launches.

00:04:29.509 --> 00:04:33.460
And time is of the essence - especially if
you’re Elon Musk and your dream is to build

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:36.659
a city on Mars with a fleet of 1,000 ships.

00:04:36.659 --> 00:04:42.289
Before ever taking off on this perilous journey
with people aboard, Starship will fly uncrewed

00:04:42.289 --> 00:04:45.659
many, many times - perfecting launching and
landing.

00:04:45.659 --> 00:04:50.470
The real success will be determined if and
when launches and landings become as common

00:04:50.470 --> 00:04:51.879
as a plane ride.

00:04:51.879 --> 00:04:56.400
Behind this vision of the future are incredible
engineers who are solving the problems needed

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:58.600
to make the Mars dream a reality.

